Sensitivity analyses
The planned sensitivity analyses test whether the result depends on particular choices:

a sequential target-trial emulation, which compares people newly diagnosed in each month with everyone else eligible that month. The comparison group is weighted by the odds of diagnosis from a propensity score rather than matched, with weights truncated at the 1st and 99th percentiles and untruncated results also reported;
the other way of handling death (D8);
outcome models on the dollar scale and on log cost;
a stricter claims definition of cryptococcosis;
one control per case, exact matching on cytomegalovirus infection, a narrower propensity score caliper under Option B, and the matching method not chosen as primary;
recipients of a first transplant only;
diagnoses before and after the October 2015 change in diagnosis codes.

Specified in: §9 Sensitivity analyses, Appendix A.

The United States Renal Data System
The United States Renal Data System (USRDS) is a national data system on chronic kidney disease and kidney failure in the United States, funded by the National Institute of Diabetes and Digestive and Kidney Diseases. It links three kinds of record under one patient identifier1:

registry records for people with kidney failure, including dates of dialysis, transplant and death;
transplant records from the Organ Procurement and Transplantation Network (OPTN), for kidney and kidney–pancreas recipients;
Medicare enrollment and claims.

Why cost data are hard to model
Monthly cost data have three awkward features12:

many months have no cost at all;
a few months, usually those with a hospital stay, cost a great deal;
the distribution is therefore strongly skewed to the right.

The quantity of interest is the mean cost, because the total spent equals the mean times the number of patients. Transforming costs to a log scale makes them easier to model but estimates something else (roughly a geometric mean), and converting back to dollars needs extra steps that can be biased13,14. Models that describe the mean directly avoid this15.

Models for costs
A generalized linear model for monthly cost can include:

a log link, so that the model describes the logarithm of the mean cost rather than the mean of logged costs;
a Tweedie distribution, which allows exact zeros and a long right tail in the same model16;
the logarithm of the number of days observed as an offset, so that a partly observed month counts in proportion to its length.

Months from the same person, and people in the same matched set, are not independent. Generalized estimating equations give standard errors that allow for this17, clustered on the matched set, and also on the patient if one person can appear in more than one matched set. A cluster bootstrap gives a confidence interval by resampling whole matched sets with replacement and repeating the whole calculation each time18.

Earlier USRDS studies of cost after an event
Other studies have estimated excess Medicare payments after an event in kidney transplant recipients using USRDS data, each with a different design:

pneumonia, sepsis and urinary tract infection in the first year after transplant, as marginal first-year costs19;
post-transplant lymphoproliferative disease, as costs per patient-year, with Medicare eligibility limited to three years after transplant for most recipients20;
dementia, compared with propensity-matched recipients at the equivalent time after transplant, as monthly and cumulative costs21.

This study’s design and how it differs are described under Study design and methods.
